<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  ADDED. The frontend does not inject EJBs; it looks them up remotely through
  ServiceLookupUtil, which builds names of the form

      ejb:/admin-backend//<Service>!<FQCN>

  and 73 service producers route through that. Without an EJB client context the
  lookup resolves to nothing:

      NoSuchEJBException: EJBCLIENT000079: Unable to discover destination for
      request for EJB StatelessEJBLocator for "/admin-backend/RapportService",
      view is interface ...RapportService, affinity is None

  Both WARs are deployed in the same server, so the local receiver can serve
  these calls without any network hop. Declaring it here is what tells the client
  that.

  local-receiver-pass-by-value MUST stay true. Setting it to false was tried
  first, to avoid serialising arguments for a call that never leaves the JVM, and
  it fails immediately:

      java.lang.ClassCastException: no.valg.eva.admin.rbac.domain.model.Operator
      cannot be cast to no.valg.eva.admin.rbac.domain.model.Operator

  The two WARs each bundle their own admin-common, so the same class name is
  loaded by two classloaders. Pass-by-value serialises across the boundary and
  the copy is rebuilt in the caller's classloader; pass-by-reference hands over
  an object the caller's classloader cannot accept. The cost of serialisation is
  what keeps the two deployments independent.

  The release must have carried an equivalent descriptor, or configured the
  client elsewhere; it was not published. See NF-009.
-->
<jboss-ejb-client xmlns="urn:jboss:ejb-client:1.2">
    <client-context>
        <ejb-receivers local-receiver-pass-by-value="true"/>
    </client-context>
</jboss-ejb-client>
